Testing the driver without the adapter
======================================

A fake FL2000 + IT66121 in userspace (fl2000-emu.c), on raw_gadget and dummy_hcd, in
a VM whose root file system is the host's, read only. No root is needed. It answers
the register, I2C and EDID traffic like the chip, takes frames on the bulk endpoint
and sends interrupts. Nothing here says anything about the real chip: timings, DAC,
the IT66121 itself, imports from a GPU or a compositor. What it is good for is what
costs a reboot or a crash on the real thing: unplug, unbind, rmmod, USB resets, errors
and the frame layout, under KASAN, lockdep, kmemleak and fault injection.

Kernel (a checkout with the driver in drivers/gpu/drm/fl2000/ and its Kconfig hook,
such as what scripts/make-upstream-series.sh leaves, and with a clean source tree):

    make O=$KB x86_64_defconfig
    cat scripts/vm-test/kernel.config >> $KB/.config
    make O=$KB olddefconfig
    make O=$KB -j8 bzImage modules
    make O=$KB INSTALL_MOD_PATH=$MODS modules_install

Run, with qemu-system-x86_64 (/dev/kvm must be accessible) and modetest, libdrm and
python3 on the host:

    scripts/vm-test/run.sh $KB $MODS /tmp/vm.log all
    grep -E '^(###|FAIL)' /tmp/vm.log

The last line of the suite is "### RESULT: n failures". The panic after it is init
exiting while the VM powers off. Suites: all and archall (no fault injection, for a
kernel without it), and what they are made of, see the end of init.sh. Standing alone:
race; fbcon, with fbcon=map:0; two, with dummy_hcd.num=2; and suspfail, with a module
where drm_mode_config_helper_suspend() is made to fail.

To run the kernel the machine runs, with the very module that gets installed, if its
distribution builds dummy_hcd, raw_gadget and 9p: see mk-distro-initrd.sh. The result of
both is in docs/vm-test-v2.log.

To run another build of the module, say an older version to see a test catch a bug:

    make -C $SRC O=$KB M=$PWD/old KBUILD_MODPOST_WARN=1 CONFIG_DRM_FL2000=m modules
    scripts/vm-test/run.sh $KB $MODS /tmp/vm.log huge fl_ko=$PWD/old/fl2000.ko

The suites huge, i2c0 and oldrmmod print what happens instead of judging it. v1 of the
driver crashes in all three.

fl2000-t.c drives the DRM device: atomic commits with a pattern in the framebuffer,
which verify_frame.py compares with the frame that reached the endpoint, including the
word swap; a mode that would overflow the buffer size; commits while the
device goes away; allocation failures through /proc/thread-self/fail-nth.
